{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/test/test_dx-docker.py::TestDXDocker.test_dx_docker_add_to_applet", "what_it_does": "Makes a temporary applet directory, pulls busybox again, then runs dx-docker add-to-applet once expecting a parse failure and once to bundle the busybox image into the applet resources.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [130], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "setUpClass already pulls busybox at line 68, nothing in the class removes it, and add-to-applet only runs docker save on the local image (local_image_to_aci in src/python/scripts/dx-docker), so the pull at line 130 repeats a registry manifest request for an image already on disk and changes no input or output. Removing the line is a local edit."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/test/test_dx-docker.py::TestDXDocker.test_dx_docker_create_asset", "what_it_does": "Creates a temporary project and folder, runs dx-docker create-asset on ubuntu:14.04 and busybox, and checks the names of the uploaded assets.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [142], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "setUpClass pulls ubuntu:14.04 at line 67, and create-asset reads only the local image through local_image_to_aci, so the pull at line 142 repeats a registry request for an image already on disk. If the setUpClass pull failed, the class would error before this test runs, so no failure path is lost."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/test/test_dx_completion.py::TestDXTabCompletion.tearDown", "what_it_does": "After each test, deletes everything in the shared test project by looping over project_remove_folder until the server reports completion, then restores the environment variables.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [70, 71, 75], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The loop is the batched removal protocol that dxproject.py remove_folder (lines 183 to 210) and dx.py rm and rmdir also use, so each pass is server side deletion work rather than a poll, and a sleep would add only idle wall clock time. Dropping the per-test cleanup would let the 14 tests share state, which the test does not allow."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/test/test_dx_bash_helpers.py::TestDXJobutilAddOutput.test_auto", "what_it_does": "Writes 15 values into one JSON file, each through its own dx-jobutil-add-output process, then reads the file back and checks every key.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [658, 659], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Each invocation is the production path, one name and value per process with its own argument parsing, exit code and flock guarded file update (src/python/scripts/dx-jobutil-add-output), and the script has no batch form. Merging the calls would need a different test or a change to the production script, which lies outside this unit."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/test/test_dx_bash_helpers.py::TestDXJobutilAddOutput.test_auto_array", "what_it_does": "Appends each of 15 values twice to arrays in one JSON file, each append through its own dx-jobutil-add-output process, then checks the resulting arrays.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [672, 673, 674], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The 30 invocations exercise the same production command per output, with its own argument parsing, exit code and flock guarded update, and the script has no batch form. Merging them would test a different program path or require changing the production script, which is outside this unit."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/test/test_dx_bash_helpers.py::TestDXJobutilNewJob.assertNewJobInputHash", "what_it_does": "Runs dx-jobutil-new-job in test mode for one argument snippet and compares the printed input JSON with the expected hash.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [748, 895], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The helper is the process boundary for each case in test_input and for the callers in test_job_arguments and test_extra_arguments (lines 963, 970, 977), and each case has its own argument list, name lookups and environment. Merging cases into one process would exercise a different path from the one each case states, and each process costs only one short Python start."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/test/test_dx_bash_helpers.py::TestDXBashHelpers.test_deepdirs", "what_it_does": "Runs the deepdirs test applet as a job, checks four output keys for their file counts, and checks that the files in six folders carry the expected names.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [507, 509, 512], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "list_folder with describe set to True returns each object's describe, which includes class and name, so the per-object get_handler and handler.name calls in verify_files_in_dir remove about 17 per-file describe requests while the six folder listings stay as they are. The class field replaces the isinstance check on DXFile, and the raised error and its text stay the same. This repair needs the DNAnexus API and is accepted on code merits."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/test/mock_api/api.py::set_payload", "what_it_does": "Builds a fresh 512 MiB random payload for the mock file server on each POST, keeps it for range downloads, and records its md5 and size in the file description.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [27, 29, 30, 31, 35], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Measured in this session: joining the 1024 blocks once gives identical bytes and md5 for a fixed seed, but its median build time was 1.77 s against 1.49 s for the current BytesIO and getvalue build, over three runs each, so the copy removal does not save energy. A getbuffer variant would change the type of app.payload that serve_download slices, which lies outside this unit."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/test/test_dx_symlink.py::md5_checksum", "what_it_does": "Returns the md5 hex digest of a local file by running the md5sum binary found on PATH and parsing its output.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [58, 64], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "No file in the repository calls md5_checksum, since a grep for the name finds only its definition, so the md5sum fork it describes never runs and no saving exists. An in-process hash would also remove the md5sum-missing and failure exits that the function owns, which changes its behaviour."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/test/test_dxabs.py::TestDXFile.test_upload_download_large_file_size_dxfile", "what_it_does": "Writes about 104 MB of random bytes to a local file, uploads it with a 100 MB write buffer to check the two part split, then downloads it and compares the copies.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [85, 86], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "On Python 3 the unit cannot run, because the text mode read of urandom at line 86 raises UnicodeDecodeError (a 4 KB sample from the same interpreter failed the same way), and setUpClass writes a str to a binary temporary file at line 56. On Python 2 text and binary modes are identical, so there is no decode work to remove. The crash is a separate defect to report."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/test/test_dxasset.py::TestDXBuildAsset.test_build_asset_with_valid_dxasset", "what_it_does": "Runs dx build_asset --json on a dxasset.json directory, then checks the bundle's project and the instance type of the job that created it.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [126, 127], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "The same record is described twice in a row, and the second call reads only createdBy, which the first default describe already returned. Keeping the first result and indexing both fields from it removes one describe request and keeps the same assertions. This repair needs the DNAnexus API and is accepted on code merits."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/test/test_dx_bash_helpers.py::TestDXBashHelpersBenchmark.run_applet_with_flags", "what_it_does": "Uploads a generated A.txt into a temporary project, builds the benchmark applet with the local bash helpers, and runs it with num_files references to that file.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [581, 582], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The enclosing class is skipped at class level (line 561), so the unit never runs, and create_file_of_size at line 572 writes a str to a binary file, which fails on Python 3 before any upload. On the code merits, sharing one upload across tests needs state carried between temporary projects, which would change the isolation that temporary_project provides, and the applet run is the larger cost."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/test/test_nextflow.py::TestNextflowOfflineMode::_run_bash", "what_it_does": "Runs one snippet in a fresh bash that sources the generated nextflow applet script with a stub dx on PATH, and returns stdout and the list of described IDs.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [642], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Each case needs a clean process for its environment (NXF_OFFLINE, run options, stub call log) and for set -e behaviour. One spawn that sources the template took a median of 6 ms over 20 runs here, so a persistent shell would add protocol code for a small saving. The decisive checks are the per-call environment block at lines 623 to 639 and the subprocess.run call at line 642."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/test/test_nextflow.py::TestNextflowOfflineMode::test_quoted_offline_tokens", "what_it_does": "Checks five quoted spellings of the -offline token, asserting the NXF_OFFLINE value and the rewritten run options for each.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [866, 869], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "The two calls run setup_offline_mode on identical inputs, so one bash run that prints the run options and NXF_OFFLINE, separated by a character that cannot occur in these values, gives both results. That removes five bash spawns and their stub calls per test run, and the assertions and expected values stay the same. The saving is a few tens of milliseconds."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/test/test_nextflow.py::TestNextflowOfflineMode::test_opt_out_also_drops_bare_offline", "what_it_does": "For three cases, runs setup_offline_mode once to read the run options and once to read NXF_OFFLINE, and checks both values.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [849, 852], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Both runs execute setup_offline_mode with the same describe output and environment, so one bash process that prints the run options and NXF_OFFLINE, separated by a character absent from these cases, gives both values. That removes one bash spawn and one set of dx stub calls per case, a saving of milliseconds per test run."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/test/test_nextflow.py::TestNextflowOfflineMode::test_setup_offline_mode_fails_open", "what_it_does": "Checks that when the outbound internet query fails, NXF_OFFLINE stays unset and the run prints that it could not determine the state.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [904, 906], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "The two calls differ only in what they print, and setup_offline_mode writes the could not determine message to stdout, so one run that lets that message through and then echoes NXF_OFFLINE as the last line satisfies both assertions. That removes one bash spawn and one set of failing stub calls, a saving of milliseconds."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/test/test_nextflow.py::TestNextflowVersionResolution::test_asset_files_valid_json_with_regions", "what_it_does": "For each of the two manifest versions, parses the three referenced asset JSON files and checks that each maps `aws:us-east-1` to a record ID.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [220, 221], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The loop reads six small files per run, a few microseconds of I/O and parsing each, which is too little to justify a cache. A module level cache would add shared state to a test that is meant to read the files as shipped."}]}

{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/dxpy/bindings/dxfile.py::DXFile.__iter__", "what_it_does": "Yields the lines of a remote file opened in text mode by reading fixed-size chunks through read and splitting the accumulated buffer on newlines.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [327, 328], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "A partial line is re-split on every chunk, so a line longer than the read chunk (16 MiB by default) costs quadratic scanning. A repair that keeps a list of pending pieces and splits only when a chunk holds a newline, found with rfind, scans each byte once and yields the same lines and the same trailing piece, and the saving touches only lines longer than the read chunk."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/dxpy/bindings/dxfile_functions.py::_download_dxfile", "what_it_does": "Downloads the chunks of a remote file in parallel into a local file, checks each part against its md5 or checksum, and returns False so download_dxfile can retry after a recoverable part error.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [420, 461], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "On a retry the rb+ branch re-reads and re-hashes every part already on disk, but skipping that check would change the result when the local file is altered between attempts, because the check truncates the file at the first part that no longer matches. Skipping only parts verified in the same call sequence needs new state threaded through a private helper that test_dxfile_download.py calls directly, so the repair would not preserve behaviour on every input."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/dxpy/bindings/dxfile_functions.py::upload_local_file", "what_it_does": "Uploads a local file or file-like object into a new platform file in buffered parts and retries the whole upload up to twice when the parts do not finish closing.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [646, 653, 680, 688], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Each retry creates a new file object and re-uploads the whole local file, but wait_until_parts_uploaded raises DXError only after its 255 second timeout and reports no failed part, so the retry cannot be narrowed to the missing parts. Reusing the first handler would change the returned file ID and leave the abandoned file on the platform."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/dxpy/bindings/dxfile_functions.py::_verify", "what_it_does": "Checks the md5 of a local file by running the md5sum executable found on PATH and exits through err_exit on a mismatch or a failed run.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [161, 167], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The subprocess costs one fork and exec per symlink download, which is small next to reading and hashing the file, and both paths read the file once. Switching to md5_hasher would also remove the missing md5sum exit and change the mismatch message, which prints a bytes repr of the digest today, so the observable output would change."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/dxpy/bindings/dxglobalworkflow.py::DXGlobalWorkflow.describe_underlying_workflow", "what_it_does": "Returns the workflow description for one region of a global workflow by describing its regional underlying workflow, with a per-region cache that never hits.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [181, 194], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The write at line 194 replaces the whole dict, so the check at line 181 always misses, but every caller in the repo, exec_io.py line 433, dx.py line 3461 through append_underlying_workflow_desc, and executable_unbuilder.py line 372, runs this once per object, so the repair would avoid no describe call. A cache hit would also return a stale description when describe_output differs, because the cache check runs before describe_output is read."}, {"label": "b", "lines": [181, 226], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "After get_underlying_workflow runs on the same handle, line 226 leaves a DXWorkflow object in _workflow_desc_by_region, and the membership test at line 181 then raises TypeError, which a stand-in class confirms because DXWorkflow defines no container dunder. That is a crash rather than wasted work, and removing line 226 would change the exception on that input, so it belongs in a correctness fix."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/dxpy/bindings/dxglobalworkflow.py::DXGlobalWorkflow.get_underlying_workflow", "what_it_does": "Returns a DXWorkflow handle for the underlying workflow of one region, describing the global workflow first when no description is passed in.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [215, 226, 227], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "The cache _workflows_by_region is read at line 215 but never written, so each stage-qualified input in exec_io.py line 777 builds a new handle whose first stages access costs one workflow describe, and caching dxid-based handles by region removes those repeats since globalworkflow IDs are immutable (name and alias handles stay uncached because an alias can move between calls). Cache hits skip the global describe and the region check, so a later loss of access goes unreported by this lookup while the run call still checks it, and line 226 stays as it is."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/dxpy/bindings/dxglobalworkflow.py::DXGlobalWorkflow._get_run_input", "what_it_does": "Builds the run input for a global workflow by looking up the region of the target project and delegating to the underlying workflow for that region.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [262], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "A project region does not change, so the project_describe at line 262 repeats on every run through the same handle, and caching the region per non-None project in a dict created in __init__ removes that request. The saving is one small API call per repeated run and none for the one-shot CLI, and a cache hit skips the access check for that project, which the run call still makes."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/dxpy/bindings/dxjob.py::DXJob.wait_on_done", "what_it_does": "Polls one job state describe per interval until the job is done, has failed, or the timeout passes.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [311, 339, 340], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "The loop sends a describe every fixed interval, 2 seconds by default, for up to seven days, so a one-hour job makes about 1800 requests. Sleeping min(interval times 2 to the i, max of interval and 128), the same cap that DXDataObject._wait_on_close in bindings/__init__.py uses, cuts an hour to about 35 requests, and keeping interval as the floor and adding the real sleep to elapsed leaves the timeout, the return value, and the exceptions unchanged, at the cost of up to 128 seconds of extra latency after completion."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/dxpy/bindings/dxproject.py::DXContainer.remove_folder", "what_it_does": "Removes a folder through repeated partial removal requests until the server reports the removal completed.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [206, 213], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "With partial set to true each request removes a batch of objects on the server, so the requests are not redundant, and a sleep would lengthen wall-clock time without lowering the request count. The repo gives no sign of a response that reports completed false without progress, and that is the only case where the back-to-back loop would waste energy."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/dxpy/bindings/search.py::_find", "what_it_does": "Wraps a system find call in a generator that pages through results until the consumer limit or the final page is reached.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [97, 98, 104], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "When the limit is reached exactly at the end of a page that has a next token, the generator fetches the following page after the consumer takes the last allowed item, because the limit check runs only at the next yield. Testing num_results == limit right after each yield returns before that request and yields the same items, so a consumer such as list sees no difference."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/dxpy/bindings/dxworkflow.py::DXWorkflow.add_stage", "what_it_does": "Adds an executable as a stage through workflow_add_stage, then refreshes the cached workflow description in a finally block.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [242], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The describe refreshes _desc, which __getattr__ serves for editVersion, stages, and every other key, so a describe restricted to editVersion and stages would drop the other keys and make later reads raise AttributeError. Deferring the refresh with _desc set to an empty dict would save one describe per chain of mutations only, and it would move any describe error from this call to a later read."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/dxpy/bindings/dxworkflow.py::DXWorkflow.remove_stage", "what_it_does": "Removes a stage through workflow_remove_stage, then refreshes the cached workflow description in a finally block.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [257, 274], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The describe at line 274 is the only refresh of _desc after the mutation, and later editVersion and stages reads depend on it, so a restricted describe would drop keys. Deferring it would save one describe per chain of mutations only, for the same reasons given for add_stage."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/dxpy/bindings/dxworkflow.py::DXWorkflow.move_stage", "what_it_does": "Moves a stage to a new index through workflow_move_stage, then refreshes the cached workflow description in a finally block.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [277, 295], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The describe at line 295 is the only refresh of _desc after the mutation, and later editVersion and stages reads depend on it, so a restricted describe would drop keys. Deferring it would save one describe per chain of mutations only, for the same reasons given for add_stage."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/dxpy/bindings/dxworkflow.py::DXWorkflow.update", "what_it_does": "Applies metadata changes such as title, summary, and stages to a workflow through workflow_update, then refreshes the cached description when any change was requested.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [297, 365], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The describe at line 365 is the only refresh of _desc after the mutation, and later editVersion and stages reads depend on it, so a restricted describe would drop keys. Deferring it would save one describe per chain of mutations only, for the same reasons given for add_stage."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/dxpy/bindings/dxworkflow.py::DXWorkflow.update_stage", "what_it_does": "Changes a stage executable and or its metadata through up to two workflow update calls, refreshing the cached description after each one.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [367, 412, 415, 434, 437], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The describe at line 415 supplies the editVersion that the second call at line 435 uses, so only the describe at line 437 could go, and removing it would leave a stale editVersion in _desc for the next mutation. Deferring it would save one describe only for calls that change both the executable and the metadata."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/dxpy/bindings/dxworkflow.py::DXWorkflow._get_stage_id", "what_it_does": "Resolves a stage index, stage ID, or stage name to a stage ID by reading the cached stages list.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [183, 185, 190], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The regex compile is served from the re module cache and a stage list holds tens of entries, so the per-call cost is microseconds. The name branch needs the full list to detect duplicate names and raise, so an early stop would change the error on that input."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/dxpy/cli/dataset_utilities.py::extract_assay_expression", "what_it_does": "Resolves a dataset or cohort record, selects a molecular expression assay, validates the filters, and writes expression data fetched from vizserver to the output file.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [1468, 1475, 1566, 1571, 1578, 1664], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Dataset.assays_info_dict and descriptor_file_dict each download and parse the descriptor on every access, which happens six times without --assay-name and four times with it. Memoizing the parsed descriptor inside descriptor_file_dict in src/python/dxpy/bindings/apollo/dataset.py cuts those fetches to one, since every reader builds new structures from it and nothing mutates it, so the outputs stay the same."}, {"label": "b", "lines": [1662], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "DATASET_DESCRIPTOR is assigned here and never read in this function or anywhere else in the repo, so the line is one more full descriptor fetch for nothing. Removing it changes behaviour only if this one download fails while the earlier fetches of the same file succeeded."}, {"label": "c", "lines": [1401, 1433], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "resolve_existing_path describes the record with default fields at line 1401, and resolve_cohort_to_dataset describes it again at line 1433 to get properties and details that the first call lacks. Asking for those fields in the first describe, which accepts a describe dict and passes it to the find call at search.py line 259, and giving resolve_cohort_to_dataset an optional precomputed description that its one-argument test callers can omit, removes one describe request per path invocation."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/dxpy/cli/dataset_utilities.py::validate_cohort_ids", "what_it_does": "Converts the supplied cohort IDs to the key type of the primary entity, queries vizserver for the matching rows, and returns the converted list along with the converter lambda.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [1758], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "The reduce with a+[int(b)] or its float and str variants copies the accumulated list at every step, so N IDs cost about N squared over two element copies, and 50,000 string IDs took 11.7 seconds in a timing run here against 0.005 seconds for a comprehension. A list comprehension over the same converter returns the same list and raises the same ValueError on bad input, and the returned lambda is kept because generate_pheno_filter in src/python/dxpy/dx_extract_utils/cohort_filter_payload.py uses it at lines 37 and 39, where the same quadratic reduce runs and needs its own repair."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/dxpy/cli/dataset_utilities.py::DXDatasetDictionary.create_coding_name_dframe", "what_it_does": "Builds a coding dataframe with one row per code, carrying meaning, concept, and display order, and walks a node tree when the coding is hierarchical.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [2292, 2295], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "The membership test and the index call each scan the display list for every code, so the cost grows as codes times display length, and 8,000 codes took 1.05 seconds in a timing run here. A dict of first positions built once, with setdefault so that the first index wins as index does, gives the same display_order values, and the repair runs only when display is a list, so a malformed display still takes the original path and raises the same error."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/dxpy/cli/dataset_utilities.py::DXDatasetDictionary.load_data_dictionary", "what_it_does": "Builds one entity dataframe per entity, then writes relationship and reference fields into the source entity frame for each join edge.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [2068, 2070], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "The result of assign is bound to a local that is never read, since the next pass of the loop rebinds source_eblock from eblocks, so each edge copies a whole entity frame for nothing. Dropping those lines leaves the in-place writes through values at lines 2061 to 2066 unchanged, and assign never modifies its input, so eblocks ends in the same state."}, {"label": "b", "lines": [2057], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The full-column comparison runs once per edge and feeds both the uniqueness check at line 2058 and the write at line 2063, so it cannot be removed. A vector comparison over one entity field rows costs microseconds per edge, so the energy involved is negligible."}]}

{"step": 2, "unit": "src/python/dxpy/cli/dataset_utilities.py::get_codings", "what_it_does": "Pages through the viz-meta codings endpoint, merges coding fragments that the server split across pages, rebuilds split hierarchies, and returns all codings keyed by name.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [278], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "The page dict is local to one pass of the loop and nothing else holds it, so the deepcopy at line 278 duplicates every coding tree for no reader. Storing the page object directly returns the same content with the same requests, and the only divergence is for a transport that hands back an object the caller still holds, which JSON decoding never does."}]}
